    },
    {
      "id": "3NsISwq0XngzvPH7gViZE",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -48245.89204893049,
      "y": -5259.9144308073355,
      "width": 1907.7586669921875,
      "height": 325,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a8",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 716038596,
      "version": 989,
      "versionNonce": 1141952196,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784262701838,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to cut down the number of queries to the DB?\n\n1. Good Solution : Cache the Top K for each time window\n- Put the query result in a Redis Cache with a TTL of couple of hours or even less if more precision required\n- Key : top-k:{window}:{truncated_timestamp} and value will be list of videos and views.\n- Most client queries returned in < 1ms\nProblem : If cache expires, we have thundering herd could be solved using coalescing requests(allowing only one request to go to db and refill the cache and other requests read from cache after)\nBut our top k query itself takes longer to run so requirement not met.\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Precompute Top-K for each time window\n- Add a cron job Top-K Cron that populates the cache periodically before cache's TTL, this will allow all queries to return within 10-100ms\nProblem : more complexity, what if cron job fails",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to cut down the number of queries to the DB?\n\n1. Good Solution : Cache the Top K for each time window\n- Put the query result in a Redis Cache with a TTL of couple of hours or even less if more precision required\n- Key : top-k:{window}:{truncated_timestamp} and value will be list of videos and views.\n- Most client queries returned in < 1ms\nProblem : If cache expires, we have thundering herd could be solved using coalescing requests(allowing only one request to go to db and refill the cache and other requests read from cache after)\nBut our top k query itself takes longer to run so requirement not met.\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Precompute Top-K for each time window\n- Add a cron job Top-K Cron that populates the cache periodically before cache's TTL, this will allow all queries to return within 10-100ms\nProblem : more complexity, what if cron job fails",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "r2jaBBXtxxwG6HzG40okt",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -48237.40778952684,
      "y": -4843.868538621113,
      "width": 2790.998046875,
      "height": 625,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a9",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1465685316,
      "version": 2328,
      "versionNonce": 618420164,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784263933711,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to handle massive writes to DB?\n- 70B views/day / (100k seconds/day) = 700k writes/sec\n- Modern RDBMS can handle 10k+ writes per sec per node\n- Videos/Day = 1 hour content/second / (6 minutes content/video) * (100k seconds/day) = 1M videos/day\n  Total Videos = 1M videos/day * 365 days/year * 10 years = 3.6B videos\n- Naive Storage = 4B videos * (8 bytes/ID + 8 bytes/count) = 64 GB\n\nSharding Ingestion\n- Our views pipeline ViewEvent(Kafka) is already partitioned by videoId, we scale our View Consumer horizontally to read from each partition of ViewEvent topic.\n- Shard DB and each ViewConsumer will be writing to its own shard of DB\n- Given our scale, we'll need 70 DB shared instances, but we now no longer can get our SQL query output from a single query as multi shard queries are involved.\n- So we query each shared for their top-k, and merge results from each shard to finally returned topk from the merged list\n\n\nBatching Ingestion\n- Even though we have 3.6B videos but view will be happening to a very small subset of videos say the popular ones.\n- Instead of writing each view to the DB, we batch up the writes for each video and flush them periodically to DB\n- Flink : A stream processing framework giving tools for batching & aggregation in streaming applications. This is a great option here.\n- It handles checkpoint and recovery for us, as if any node goes down it can rewind to that checkpoint in kafka and resume processing from there, no data loss.\n- We use BoundedOutOfOrdernessWatermarkStrategy to handle late events: basically we'll tell Flink that we're ok waiting up to some time (probably 30 seconds here, < 1 minute) for late events to arrive.\n- We'll also use a tumbling window of 1 hour to aggregate the views for each video.\n- Why this? say you went down for 45mins, and you came back up but you'll be occupied in processing current incoming views what about the ones which happened in that 45mins window(the one to be replayed from kafka again), those will be processed partially with time with some delay\n- So, our flink is accepting individual view events and outputting sums of views per video on 1hr interval.\n- DB handles bulk updates much better, also our writes became smaller as we're summing up views for 1hr\n- This allows us to bring our shards down to 5-10",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to handle massive writes to DB?\n- 70B views/day / (100k seconds/day) = 700k writes/sec\n- Modern RDBMS can handle 10k+ writes per sec per node\n- Videos/Day = 1 hour content/second / (6 minutes content/video) * (100k seconds/day) = 1M videos/day\n  Total Videos = 1M videos/day * 365 days/year * 10 years = 3.6B videos\n- Naive Storage = 4B videos * (8 bytes/ID + 8 bytes/count) = 64 GB\n\nSharding Ingestion\n- Our views pipeline ViewEvent(Kafka) is already partitioned by videoId, we scale our View Consumer horizontally to read from each partition of ViewEvent topic.\n- Shard DB and each ViewConsumer will be writing to its own shard of DB\n- Given our scale, we'll need 70 DB shared instances, but we now no longer can get our SQL query output from a single query as multi shard queries are involved.\n- So we query each shared for their top-k, and merge results from each shard to finally returned topk from the merged list\n\n\nBatching Ingestion\n- Even though we have 3.6B videos but view will be happening to a very small subset of videos say the popular ones.\n- Instead of writing each view to the DB, we batch up the writes for each video and flush them periodically to DB\n- Flink : A stream processing framework giving tools for batching & aggregation in streaming applications. This is a great option here.\n- It handles checkpoint and recovery for us, as if any node goes down it can rewind to that checkpoint in kafka and resume processing from there, no data loss.\n- We use BoundedOutOfOrdernessWatermarkStrategy to handle late events: basically we'll tell Flink that we're ok waiting up to some time (probably 30 seconds here, < 1 minute) for late events to arrive.\n- We'll also use a tumbling window of 1 hour to aggregate the views for each video.\n- Why this? say you went down for 45mins, and you came back up but you'll be occupied in processing current incoming views what about the ones which happened in that 45mins window(the one to be replayed from kafka again), those will be processed partially with time with some delay\n- So, our flink is accepting individual view events and outputting sums of views per video on 1hr interval.\n- DB handles bulk updates much better, also our writes became smaller as we're summing up views for 1hr\n- This allows us to bring our shards down to 5-10",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
