      "height": 750,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aD",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 68654716,
      "version": 2685,
      "versionNonce": 956747972,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784266356753,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- we end up processing 100 of GB of data, working time becomes minutes/hours not seconds, this could me moved out of RDBMS to spark/Hadoop but still inefficient, could do parallel processing but then excessive capacity.\n\n\n1. Good Solution : Aggregate at Coarser Granularity\n- Keeping track of each hour views per video we can keep track of per day.\n- When querying for a month we just aggregate daily data instead of hourly\n- group hour views by day and day views by month and store them to a new table in DB\n- Note this operation here is done by a separate CRON job that runs on the Views table that stores aggregation by hour\nProblem : Additional Cron Job, still slow to query the DB for this\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Maintain Aggregates for each window in DB pushed via flink(Don't understand the article)\n- Our Flink jobs will continue to aggregate, at hour grain, the views for each video.\n- Whenever a new hour has passed and we're ready to write to our database, we'll make updates to our window tables (e.g. VideoViewsLastHour) rather than writing to the hour-grain VideoViews table.\n\nI don't understand this! why don't we aggregate in flink and write to a single new table\n\nProblems : Need to write to 4 tables instead of 1, Optimise bulk operations in Postgres, use unlogged tables to avoid writing to WAL\ndelay fsync to the end of txn, choose right batch size\n\n\n3. Great Solution : Do Aggregations in Memory with Flink (With Caveats)\n- Use flink's native distributed state management to keep aggregates in Flink rather than writing the views to DB\n- Use external storage like RocksDB to move state off the help onto disk to scale\n- By maintaining state inside DB we can get entirely rid of Postgres, and let Flink perform the Top-K calculation internally\n- and write the output directly to cache at the conclusion of every minute getting rid of the top-k cron as well.\n- The rolling window aggregator is keeping in memory the count of views for each window, when window is completed it emits a VideoWindowCount that contains the window and count of views for each video in that window\n- The top-k aggregator is keeping, in memory (likely using a heap), the top K videos for a given window. It receives the VideoWindowCounts and emits a TopKResult object which contains the window and the top K videos for that window.\n- Finally write TopKResult to Redis using a sink\nProblems : It relies heavily on Flink which is not known by every interviewer, might be asked to do it without Flink! or explain how internals work",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- we end up processing 100 of GB of data, working time becomes minutes/hours not seconds, this could me moved out of RDBMS to spark/Hadoop but still inefficient, could do parallel processing but then excessive capacity.\n\n\n1. Good Solution : Aggregate at Coarser Granularity\n- Keeping track of each hour views per video we can keep track of per day.\n- When querying for a month we just aggregate daily data instead of hourly\n- group hour views by day and day views by month and store them to a new table in DB\n- Note this operation here is done by a separate CRON job that runs on the Views table that stores aggregation by hour\nProblem : Additional Cron Job, still slow to query the DB for this\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Maintain Aggregates for each window in DB pushed via flink(Don't understand the article)\n- Our Flink jobs will continue to aggregate, at hour grain, the views for each video.\n- Whenever a new hour has passed and we're ready to write to our database, we'll make updates to our window tables (e.g. VideoViewsLastHour) rather than writing to the hour-grain VideoViews table.\n\nI don't understand this! why don't we aggregate in flink and write to a single new table\n\nProblems : Need to write to 4 tables instead of 1, Optimise bulk operations in Postgres, use unlogged tables to avoid writing to WAL\ndelay fsync to the end of txn, choose right batch size\n\n\n3. Great Solution : Do Aggregations in Memory with Flink (With Caveats)\n- Use flink's native distributed state management to keep aggregates in Flink rather than writing the views to DB\n- Use external storage like RocksDB to move state off the help onto disk to scale\n- By maintaining state inside DB we can get entirely rid of Postgres, and let Flink perform the Top-K calculation internally\n- and write the output directly to cache at the conclusion of every minute getting rid of the top-k cron as well.\n- The rolling window aggregator is keeping in memory the count of views for each window, when window is completed it emits a VideoWindowCount that contains the window and count of views for each video in that window\n- The top-k aggregator is keeping, in memory (likely using a heap), the top K videos for a given window. It receives the VideoWindowCounts and emits a TopKResult object which contains the window and the top K videos for that window.\n- Finally write TopKResult to Redis using a sink\nProblems : It relies heavily on Flink which is not known by every interviewer, might be asked to do it without Flink! or explain how internals work",
